\section{What makes a usable name}{

Each suggestion is checked, and the model is asked again (up to \code{retries}
times, with the reason) when a name
\itemize{
\item has more than \code{maxWords} words (a leading "a", "an" or "the" is not
counted);
\item contains the name of one of the classes (\code{labels}), also in the plural:
a sniglet is something you can see on a specimen, such as "a serrate
margin", not the species it was found on. For labels written
\code{"Common name (Genus species)"} the common name, the scientific name and
the genus are all refused, and so is the last word of a common name of
several words ("aspen" of "Quaking aspen", so "aspen bark" is refused),
unless every class ends in it or it is a general word such as "tree" or
"flower";
\item is already the name or the coined word of another sniglet. Capitals,
punctuation and a leading article make no difference: "Serrate margin"
and "a serrate margin" are the same name;
\item is no name at all ("unknown", "n/a", or nothing but punctuation).
}

A leading "has" or "lacks" is taken off first, since the key adds its own.

If the last answer is still a duplicate it gets a number (\code{"peeling bark 2"}, status \code{"suffixed"}). If it still breaks another rule, or the model
fails (an API error, a rate limit, a refusal, a reply that cannot be
read), the sniglet keeps the name it had and the row says why in \code{status}
and \code{note}; the other sniglets are not affected.
}

\section{Long runs}{

With \code{file}, the table is written after every sniglet, so a run that is
interrupted or stopped by an error leaves what it had. \code{resume = TRUE}
picks such a file up: sniglets it already has a usable name for (status
\code{"ok"} or \code{"suffixed"}) are not asked about again, the others are. Without
\code{resume} an existing file is overwritten. The folder of \code{file} must exist.
}

